const ARTICLES={

sixfactors:{k:'The terrain',title:'A block never comes from nowhere. Six things decide whether you get stuck.',dek:'The science of creative performance clusters into six factors — three you are mostly stuck with, three you can actually move. Knowing which is which is where the work starts.',read:'8 min read',aside:'The six: personality, cognition and mental-health tendencies (stable) · motivation, mood and environment (malleable).',body:`<p>“I’m blocked” sounds like a single event, as if a door swung shut. It is more like weather. A block forms on top of conditions that were already there — and those conditions are surprisingly well mapped. In her review of the creativity research, Lisa Gallay found the whole messy literature keeps clustering around <strong>six factors</strong> that raise or lower creative performance <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. The single most useful thing she did was sort them by one blunt question: <em>can you actually change this, or are you mostly stuck with it?</em></p>

<h2>Three you are mostly stuck with</h2>
<p><strong>Personality.</strong> Two traits show up again and again: openness to experience (the strongest) and, more surprisingly, extraversion <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. <strong>Cognitive makeup</strong> — how your mind naturally forms and connects ideas. And <strong>mental-health tendencies</strong> a person carries. These are real, they matter, and you don’t change them on a Tuesday afternoon. The point of naming them is not to fix them; it is to design <em>around</em> them.</p>

<h2>Three you can actually move</h2>
<p>This is where the work happens. <strong>Motivation</strong> — whether the drive comes from inside (the pleasure of the thing) or outside (money, deadlines, approval). <strong>Mood</strong> — how you feel day to day. <strong>Environment</strong> — your space, your people, your setup.</p>
<p>The most robust finding in all of creativity science lives here: the <strong>intrinsic motivation principle</strong> <sup><a href="#ref-c">(c)</a></sup>. Creativity flourishes when you work for the interest and satisfaction of the work itself, and tends to suffer under expected evaluation, surveillance and competition. Which quietly explains one of the most common blocks of all:</p>
<blockquote>Blocks appear, disproportionately, at exactly the moment creative work becomes a job — when reviews, sales and comparison enter the room and crowd out the original engine.</blockquote>
<p>Mood matters too, but with a twist. When researchers split moods not just into good and bad but by <em>energy</em>, the picture cleared up <sup><a href="#ref-d">(d)</a></sup>. Positive <em>and</em> activated (excited, inspired) is the sweet spot. Low and flat (sad, drained) suppresses it. A calm good mood and an angry bad mood sit in the messy middle. Energy, not just positivity, is the ingredient.</p>

<h2>Why it changes what we do</h2>
<p>Creativity isn’t only inside the person. Csikszentmihályi’s systems view puts it at the meeting point of the individual, the domain and the field of gatekeepers who decide what gets through <sup><a href="#ref-e">(e)</a></sup> — which is why a purely internal account of a block is always incomplete. Money, isolation and no-win “be original but make it sell” messages are terrain too.</p>
<p>So before anyone talks about willpower, we look at the ground. You can’t out-discipline your own wiring — but you can almost always change your motivation, your mood and your environment. That is the difference between advice that shames and help that works.</p>

<div class="article-note">This is a practitioner synthesis of creativity research and clinical literature, not a validated diagnostic system. It’s the best current map, not the last word.</div>

<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Gallay, L. H. (2013). <em>Understanding and Treating Creative Block in Professional Artists</em> (doctoral dissertation, Alliant International University).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Batey, M. &amp; Furnham, A. (2006); Feist, G. J. (1998), on personality and creativity.</p>
<p id="ref-c"><strong>(c)</strong> Amabile, T. M. et al. (1994); Hennessey, B. A. &amp; Amabile, T. M. (2010), the intrinsic motivation principle.</p>
<p id="ref-d"><strong>(d)</strong> Baas, M., De Dreu, C. K. W. &amp; Nijstad, B. A. (2008). A meta-analysis of 25 years of mood–creativity research. <em>Psychological Bulletin, 134</em>(6).</p>
<p id="ref-e"><strong>(e)</strong> Csikszentmihályi, M. (1997). <em>Creativity: Flow and the Psychology of Discovery and Invention</em>.</p>
</div>`},

whatisit:{k:'Foundations',title:'“Creative block” is a real thing — and it’s not what most people think.',dek:'Able, willing, stuck and suffering. A working definition, the things that only look like blocks, and why it was never just an artist problem.',read:'7 min read',aside:'The working test: skill is there, desire is there, the work still won’t come — and it hurts.',body:`<p>The phrase does a lot of hiding. Two people say “I’m blocked” and mean opposite things — one is terrified to show the work, the other has no fuel left, a third is fighting a broken brief. Useful language, poor explanation. So it helps to define it properly.</p>

<h2>The definition, done properly</h2>
<p>The most defensible academic definition comes from Mike Rose: a creative block is an <strong>inability to begin or continue working for reasons other than a lack of basic skill or commitment</strong> <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Every word earns its place. Can’t produce because you never learned the craft? That’s a skill gap, not a block. Don’t care anymore? That’s a change of values. The block proper is the strange case where the skill is there, the desire is there, and the work still won’t come.</p>
<p>The neurologist Alice Flaherty added a second criterion that matters more than it looks: you are blocked when you are not producing despite being capable of it, <strong>and you are suffering because of it</strong> <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. The suffering is the whole tell.</p>
<blockquote>Someone not producing and feeling fine is probably incubating. Someone not producing and in distress is blocked. Same silence, different thing.</blockquote>

<h2>What a block is not</h2>
<p>Three boundaries keep coming up. It is <strong>not incompetence</strong> — blocked writers test just as skilled as unblocked ones <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. It is <strong>not simply finding the work hard</strong>: highly productive people complain about the difficulty just as loudly <sup><a href="#ref-c">(c)</a></sup>. And in theory it is <strong>not procrastination</strong> — though from the outside they look near-identical, because nobody sits calmly at a blank page forever. There’s also a version that hides completely: a block can show up as a “style change”, as derivative work, or as producing <em>more</em> than usual to avoid the stuck feeling rather than move through it <sup><a href="#ref-d">(d)</a></sup>.</p>

<h2>Why there’s no “accountant’s block”</h2>
<p>Here’s the part that widens the whole thing out. Flaherty asks why we never hear about doctor’s block or accountant’s block, and answers: a block is a disorder of <strong>ill-defined problems</strong>. Where the task is given and the method is largely known in advance, you don’t get blocked — you get busy. Blocks live in the open space where the problem itself has to be found and defined.</p>
<p>Which means this was never an artist thing. It’s a <em>bringing-something-into-existence</em> thing. A founder who can’t choose a direction, a researcher who can’t start writing up, someone stuck on what their work is even <em>for</em> — same open space, same failure mode. Painting isn’t the qualification. Facing a blank, undefined problem is.</p>

<div class="article-note">Lasting, painful block is a wellbeing issue worth real support, not a discipline failure. If a block is severe or persistent, that’s a reason to talk to someone, not to push harder.</div>

<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Rose, M. (1984). <em>Writer’s Block: The Cognitive Dimension</em>. Southern Illinois University Press.</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Flaherty, A. W. (2004). <em>The Midnight Disease: The Drive to Write, Writer’s Block, and the Creative Brain</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-c"><strong>(c)</strong> Boice, R. (1985). Cognitive components of blocking. <em>Written Communication, 2</em>(1).</p>
<p id="ref-d"><strong>(d)</strong> Kantor, M. (1995). <em>Understanding Writer’s Block: A Therapist’s Guide to Diagnosis and Treatment</em>.</p>
</div>`},

gallay:{k:'The evidence',title:'The 269-page study that quietly maps creative block.',dek:'The field barely exists — so one doctoral dissertation did the un-glamorous job of pulling the scattered science together. It’s the backbone of how we think.',read:'6 min read',aside:'Fun, bleak fact: a database search found only two empirical studies focused on block in professional artists — both decades old.',body:`<p>You would assume something this common has a shelf of research behind it. It doesn’t. When Lisa Gallay went looking, her database search turned up only <strong>two</strong> empirical research articles focused specifically on creative block in professional artists — Hirst and Crosson — both decades old <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup><sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup><sup><a href="#ref-c">(c)</a></sup>. Before 1991, not a single book had been written on writer’s block. So her 2013 dissertation did the boring, valuable thing: it gathered the fragments — creativity science, clinical literature, practitioner experience — into one usable map <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>

<h2>Why so little research?</h2>
<p>Gallay’s own diagnosis is worth repeating, because it’s honest. Block is a genuinely hard research target: intrapsychic, interpersonal, situational and historical factors combine differently in every person. Creativity research has no agreed measuring tools, so studies can’t be compared head to head. Samples are a jumble — students and professionals, painters and composers. And there’s a stereotype problem: artists get treated as a mysterious, underserved group rather than a population you can actually study. Into that vacuum rushed a hundred self-help books of wildly varying quality.</p>
<blockquote>A field this thin is a reason for humility, not confidence. We say “the evidence suggests”, not “science proves”.</blockquote>

<h2>What the map gave us</h2>
<p>Two things we lean on came straight out of this synthesis. First, the <strong>six factors</strong> that govern creative performance, split into stable and malleable — the terrain a block grows on. Second, the recognition that there is <strong>no canonical taxonomy</strong> of block types; as Gallay puts it, there are as many classifications as there have been explanations of the cause. That sounds like a weakness. It’s actually a permission: it lets us build a working map that’s judged by whether it helps, not by whether it matches a rulebook that doesn’t exist.</p>

<h2>The bright spot</h2>
<p>The newest and most striking data arrived in 2024, when a study finally put numbers on something practitioners always suspected: art block and burnout are almost the same construct, correlating around .84, with maladaptive perfectionism close behind <sup><a href="#ref-d">(d)</a></sup>. After forty years of near-silence, the field is finally moving — which is exactly why we treat all of this as the best current map, and expect to redraw it.</p>

<div class="article-note">We build on scarce research on purpose, and we say where it’s thin. An evidence-informed method is one that’s allowed to change its mind.</div>

<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Gallay, L. H. (2013). <em>Understanding and Treating Creative Block in Professional Artists</em> (doctoral dissertation).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Hirst, B. (1992). How artists overcome creative blocks (qualitative study of six professional artists).</p>
<p id="ref-c"><strong>(c)</strong> Crosson, C. W. (1982). Creative block: A brief inquiry. <em>The Arts in Psychotherapy, 9</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-d"><strong>(d)</strong> When the creative well dries up — burnout syndrome and art block (2024). <em>Thinking Skills and Creativity</em>.</p>
</div>`},

personality:{k:'The terrain',title:'You can’t out-discipline your own wiring — and you shouldn’t try.',dek:'Why understanding someone’s stable traits comes before fixing their block. With a personal detour through photography, spreadsheets, and giving up on being the wrong kind of creative.',read:'7 min read',aside:'The move isn’t to fight your nature. It’s to find the version of the work your nature will actually do.',body:`<p>Of the six factors that shape creative performance, three are relatively fixed — personality, how your mind processes, your mental-health tendencies <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. You don’t coach those away. But knowing them changes everything about <em>how</em> you approach a block, because the same block on different wiring needs a different response.</p>

<h2>A personal detour</h2>
<p>Here’s a version of it I know from the inside. For years I assumed being creative meant being an artist — so I tried to be one. I picked up a camera, went out to shoot, tried to live the way I thought a creative person lived. And it never quite took. What I actually loved, it turned out, was sitting at a computer for hours: researching, connecting things, archiving, building systems. My mind is analytic and strategic by default. Walking around with a camera was, by then, the <em>unnatural</em> act — I was forcing myself further and further out of the way I’m built.</p>
<blockquote>At some point I stopped dragging myself out of my nature and leaned into what came easily. The block lifted — not because I tried harder, but because I stopped fighting my own wiring.</blockquote>
<p>I still love photography. I still do it — as a hobby, with no weight on it. The point isn’t that I quit; it’s that the “block” was never a discipline failure. It was a mismatch between the work I thought I <em>should</em> do and the way my brain actually wants to work.</p>

<h2>Why traits come first</h2>
<p>The research backs the instinct. Openness and extraversion track with creativity; sociability and self-promotion track with visible artistic <em>success</em> — a genuinely uncomfortable finding for quieter temperaments <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup><sup><a href="#ref-c">(c)</a></sup>. And <strong>creative self-efficacy</strong> — your belief that you can actually do the work — is powerful enough to spin up a block on its own, or spin one down <sup><a href="#ref-d">(d)</a></sup>. If we don’t know how someone is wired, we’ll hand them advice built for a different person, and then quietly blame them when it doesn’t land.</p>

<h2>Design around, don’t override</h2>
<p>So the first job isn’t a technique — it’s understanding the person. Is the solitary version of this work draining them, when a collaborator would unlock it? Are they being told to “just be spontaneous” when they think best with structure? The fix is rarely “become someone else”. It’s to find the shape of the work that your nature will actually sustain — and to stop treating a mismatch as a moral failing.</p>

<div class="article-note">Stable traits are for designing around, not diagnosing. Where a mental-health tendency is doing the driving, that’s a reason for qualified support, not a coaching workaround.</div>

<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Gallay, L. H. (2013). <em>Understanding and Treating Creative Block in Professional Artists</em> (doctoral dissertation).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Batey, M. &amp; Furnham, A. (2006); Feist, G. J. (1998), on openness, extraversion and creativity.</p>
<p id="ref-c"><strong>(c)</strong> Abuhamdeh, S. &amp; Csikszentmihályi, M. (2004). The artistic personality: A systems perspective.</p>
<p id="ref-d"><strong>(d)</strong> Bandura, A. (1977); Tierney, P. &amp; Farmer, S. M. (2002), on self-efficacy.</p>
</div>`},

catalog:{k:'The types',title:'Every kind of creative block anyone has bothered to document.',dek:'There’s no official taxonomy — so here’s the honest catalogue. Six surveys, four personality types, and the five families they collapse into.',read:'9 min read',aside:'No canonical list exists. That’s not a gap to paper over — it’s why a working map has to earn its keep.',body:`<p>A warning before the list: there is no agreed taxonomy of block types. As Gallay notes, there are as many classifications as there have been explanations of the cause <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. So this isn’t <em>the</em> catalogue — it’s every serious attempt, laid side by side, and then the honest synthesis we actually use.</p>

<h2>Who says what</h2>
<p><strong>Hirst</strong> interviewed working artists and found three kinds: normal process blocks (the ordinary dead ends of making), psychological blocks, and post-completion blocks that strike right after a show or commission <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. <strong>Crosson</strong> surveyed 271 artists and writers and sorted the causes into six buckets — external pressures (childcare, jobs, housework — the biggest by far), emotional problems, the creative process itself, lack of discipline, physical problems, and unspecified <sup><a href="#ref-c">(c)</a></sup>. <strong>Barrios &amp; Singer</strong> ran nearly sixty tests each on 48 badly blocked professionals and found four personality profiles: the anxious self-critic, the hostile one who won’t be compared, the apathetic one who’s gone flat (the most stuck), and the angry, externally-driven one <sup><a href="#ref-d">(d)</a></sup>. <strong>Kantor</strong> argued block is usually a <em>symptom</em> of something underneath — mood, anxiety, OCD, substance use — and splits into acute (comes and goes with the cause) and chronic (breaks free and feeds itself) <sup><a href="#ref-e">(e)</a></sup>. <strong>Flaherty</strong> gives the most useful split of all: high-energy block (anxious, flooded, fleeing the desk) versus low-energy block (empty, depressive, no drive) <sup><a href="#ref-f">(f)</a></sup>. And <strong>Maisel</strong> lists some twenty contributing sources, from self-censorship to existential meaning problems <sup><a href="#ref-g">(g)</a></sup>.</p>

<h2>The five families</h2>
<p>Collapse all of that and five families remain. <strong>Cognitive</strong> — failures inside the process: rigid self-imposed rules, editing too early, fixation on a wrong approach, an unsolved problem in the work itself, over- or under-planning <sup><a href="#ref-h">(h)</a></sup>. (Mark Twain lost seven years on <em>Huckleberry Finn</em> by sending his characters up the wrong river; he changed the plot and finished in four months. Sometimes the block is a story problem, not a you problem.) <strong>Emotional</strong> — perfectionism (the single strongest correlate in modern data), fear, self-criticism, shame, the internal critic. <strong>Motivational / depletion</strong> — burnout, apathy, post-project emptiness, the intrinsic engine dying when art becomes a job. <strong>Social / environmental</strong> — life logistics, money, isolation, hostile comparison, no-win double-bind messages. <strong>Clinical / neurological</strong> — where the block is a symptom of something diagnosable, and the honest move is to refer, not to coach.</p>
<blockquote>Most people are a mix, with one family pulling hardest right now. Naming that one — honestly, not conveniently — is most of the work.</blockquote>

<h2>The one that fools everyone</h2>
<p>One thing on the emotional list quietly explains a huge share of blocks. Bayles &amp; Orland point out that an artist’s sense of self fuses with their work, so “fears of not making good enough art turn into fears of not being good enough” as a person <sup><a href="#ref-i">(i)</a></sup>. Once that happens, every blank page is a verdict on your worth — unbearable, so you avoid it. That’s not weakness. It’s a predictable trap, and it has a way out.</p>

<div class="article-note">These are working families, not diagnoses. They overlap, more than one can be live at once, and the map should change when better evidence turns up.</div>

<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Gallay, L. H. (2013). <em>Understanding and Treating Creative Block in Professional Artists</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Hirst, B. (1992). How artists overcome creative blocks.</p>
<p id="ref-c"><strong>(c)</strong> Crosson, C. W. (1982). Creative block: A brief inquiry. <em>The Arts in Psychotherapy, 9</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-d"><strong>(d)</strong> Barrios, M. V. &amp; Singer, J. L. (1981). The treatment of creative blocks. <em>Imagination, Cognition and Personality, 1</em>(1).</p>
<p id="ref-e"><strong>(e)</strong> Kantor, M. (1995). <em>Understanding Writer’s Block</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-f"><strong>(f)</strong> Flaherty, A. W. (2004). <em>The Midnight Disease</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-g"><strong>(g)</strong> Maisel, E. (2007). <em>Creativity for Life</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-h"><strong>(h)</strong> Rose, M. (1980). Rigid rules, inflexible plans, and the stifling of language. <em>CCC, 31</em>(4).</p>
<p id="ref-i"><strong>(i)</strong> Bayles, D. &amp; Orland, T. (2011). <em>Art and Fear</em>.</p>
</div>`},

fixation:{k:'What’s happening',title:'Trying harder digs the rut deeper: the truth about fixation.',dek:'When you push on a stuck problem, your brain locks onto the first approach — even when it’s wrong. Here’s why the way out is to stop.',read:'5 min read',aside:'The tell: you keep failing the same way. That’s not persistence, it’s a groove.',body:`<p>You are grinding on a problem and getting nowhere, so you do the natural thing: you push harder. It backfires. The harder you push, the more firmly your mind clamps onto the first approach it found — and if that approach is wrong, effort just deepens the rut <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. You literally can’t see the better idea, because your attention keeps snapping back to the wrong one.</p>
<h2>Why the break works</h2>
<p>Stepping away lets the wrong approach fade so a new one can surface — the answer that arrives in the shower or halfway through a walk. But two conditions matter. You have to have done the real work first, and the break has to be genuinely easy: a walk, a chore, a nap — not more hard thinking in disguise <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<blockquote>Mark Twain lost seven years on <em>Huckleberry Finn</em> by sending his characters up the wrong river. He changed the plot, let them float down the Mississippi, and finished in four months.</blockquote>
<p>Sometimes the block was never emotional. It was a structural problem in the work, and no amount of willpower was going to dissolve it — only a different angle would.</p>
<h2>What to do with it</h2>
<p>When you catch yourself failing the same way on repeat, that’s the signal to interrupt, not to double down. Change one big variable — the approach, the constraint, the order — or leave the desk entirely and come back. The instinct that says “just power through” is exactly the one making it worse <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="article-note">This is the mechanism under a Process (“the work itself”) block. If stepping away and changing the approach still yields nothing, the problem may be elsewhere on the map.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Sio, U. N. &amp; Ormerod, T. C. (2009). Does incubation enhance problem solving? A meta-analytic review of 117 studies. <em>Psychological Bulletin, 135</em>(1).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Storm, B. C. &amp; Angello, G. (2013). Escaping mental fixation: Incubation and inhibition in creative problem solving.</p>
</div>`},

selfjudge:{k:'What’s happening',title:'Your brain can’t create and judge in the same breath.',dek:'Generating ideas and criticising them fight over the same mental space. When they run at once, nothing flows — and the silence feels like proof you’re no good.',read:'5 min read',aside:'“I write a sentence and instantly delete it” is this loop, out loud.',body:`<p>Making and judging draw on the same limited workspace, and they can’t both have it. Try to create while anxiously grading yourself and the judging eats the room: output slows, the slowdown feels like evidence you’re bad, the fear grabs more space, and round it goes <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>Premature editing</h2>
<p>Mike Rose named it “premature editing” — the critic killing each idea as it’s born <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. It’s not that the critic is wrong; it’s that it clocks in at the wrong time. Nearly every clinician who has studied block puts self-criticism at the centre of most cases <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>.</p>
<blockquote>Create first with the judge switched off. Edit later, in a separate session. Never run both gears at once.</blockquote>
<h2>The fix is physical, not moral</h2>
<p>You don’t beat this by resolving to be more confident. You beat it by separating the two jobs in time: a generation session where bad ideas are allowed and nothing gets deleted, then — later — an editing session where the critic finally gets useful work to do. Freewriting and deliberately rough drafts aren’t indulgences; they’re how you keep the workspace clear long enough to make something.</p>
<div class="article-note">This underlies many Execution blocks. Where the self-judgement has fused with self-worth, it becomes an Identity block — and needs the kinder tools, not just a timer.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Rose, M. (1980). Rigid rules, inflexible plans, and the stifling of language. <em>CCC, 31</em>(4).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Kantor, M. (1995); Flaherty, A. W. (2004) — self-criticism as a central mechanism of block.</p>
</div>`},

avoidance:{k:'What’s happening',title:'Every time you escape the desk, escaping gets easier.',dek:'Sitting down feels bad. Checking your phone feels good. That tiny hit of relief is a reward — and your brain learns the lesson fast.',read:'5 min read',aside:'This is why “waiting to feel ready” never arrives: the feeling can’t improve while avoidance keeps paying out.',body:`<p>Approaching the work produces discomfort. Escaping to a chore or a feed produces instant relief. Relief is a reward, and rewarded behaviour repeats — so every avoidance quietly makes the next one more likely <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. It has nothing to do with weak character. It’s a loop, and loops are trainable.</p>
<h2>Why willpower is the wrong lever</h2>
<p>You can’t wait your way out, because the “ready” feeling never comes while the escape keeps getting reinforced. The way to retrain the loop is to make the desk safe again: shrink the task until starting is almost trivial, sit for a tiny fixed session, and reward showing up whether or not you felt like it <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>.</p>
<blockquote>Reward attendance before quality. A smaller step is not a smaller ambition — it’s how you gather evidence that the desk won’t bite.</blockquote>
<h2>Small, scheduled, rewarded</h2>
<p>Frequent, easy, rewarded reps beat rare heroic ones. That’s the whole logic behind scheduling: you’re not forcing output, you’re teaching your nervous system that starting is survivable. Do that a few dozen times and the dread loses its grip.</p>
<div class="article-note">This is the engine of the Starting &amp; Doing block, and why behaviour beats inspiration. If avoidance masks real exhaustion, treat the depletion first — pushing an empty tank makes it worse.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Flaherty, A. W. (2004). <em>The Midnight Disease</em> (on reward, relief and delaying behaviour).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Boice, R. (1983). Contingency management in writing and the appearance of creative ideas. <em>Behaviour Research and Therapy, 21</em>(5).</p>
</div>`},

spiral:{k:'What’s happening',title:'How one bad review becomes a two-year block.',dek:'Your belief that you can do the work and your actual output feed each other — up or down. Left alone, the down version compounds.',read:'5 min read',aside:'The reversal isn’t a pep talk. It’s one small, real win on the board, fast.',body:`<p>Confidence and output run on a loop. In the healthy version, a small success raises your belief, which makes you try more, which produces better work. In the broken version it runs the other way: one failure reads as “I’m no good”, so you try less, so the result is worse, which looks like more proof <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. That’s the machinery that turns a single harsh review into a block that lasts years and sometimes ends a career.</p>
<h2>Change what failure means</h2>
<p>The lever isn’t forcing positivity; it’s the meaning you assign to a stumble. Treated as information — “that approach didn’t land, adjust” — a failure feeds learning. Treated as a verdict — “this is who I am” — it feeds the downward spiral <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>.</p>
<blockquote>Momentum reverses the spiral. Get one small, genuine win on the board before you try to fix the story in your head.</blockquote>
<h2>Graded wins</h2>
<p>Because belief is built from evidence, you rebuild it with achievable, graded goals and a visible log of small wins. Even upward spirals can curdle — endless easy success breeds complacency and repetition, which is how comfortable, successful people stall too. The aim isn’t constant success; it’s a true read of what each result actually means.</p>
<div class="article-note">This sits under Identity and Depletion blocks. A collapse of drive that won’t lift with small wins can be depression — which is a reason for proper support, not a harder goal.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Lindsley, D. H., Brass, D. J. &amp; Thomas, J. B. (1995). Efficacy–performance spirals. <em>Academy of Management Review, 20</em>(3).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Bandura, A. (1977). Self-efficacy: Toward a unifying theory of behavioral change. <em>Psychological Review, 84</em>(2).</p>
</div>`},

dwelling:{k:'What’s happening',title:'There are two kinds of overthinking. Only one is helping.',dek:'“Stop overthinking” is bad advice when the thinking is doing real work. The trick is telling brooding apart from genuine reflection.',read:'5 min read',aside:'The question that sorts them: are you turning over the problem, or turning over yourself?',body:`<p>Not all dwelling is the same. <strong>Brooding</strong> — chewing on how bad you feel — drains you and traps you, and predicts nothing good. <strong>Reflective turning-over</strong> — chewing on the problem itself — keeps the idea alive so it can develop, and actually predicts more creative ideas <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Same outward stillness, opposite effects.</p>
<h2>Why the distinction matters</h2>
<p>Tell someone circling a genuine problem to “stop overthinking” and you interrupt productive incubation. Tell someone circling their own inadequacy to “keep sitting with it” and you deepen the hole. The intervention depends entirely on which one is running.</p>
<blockquote>Turning over the work? Let it run — just give it a deadline. Turning over yourself? Step in, get moving, add some self-compassion.</blockquote>
<h2>Give reflection a boundary</h2>
<p>Reflective rumination pays off best when it’s paired with deliberately delaying the decision — you let the problem simmer, then come back and choose <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Left open-ended, even useful reflection slides into brooding. So the move isn’t to think less; it’s to point the thinking at the work, and put a fence around it.</p>
<div class="article-note">Reflection on the work can look identical to avoidance from outside. The difference is whether a real problem is being turned over — and whether you ever come back to it.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Cohen, J. R. &amp; Ferrari, J. R. (2010). Take some time to think this over: rumination, indecision, and creativity. <em>Creativity Research Journal, 22</em>(1).</p>
</div>`},

modes:{k:'What’s happening',title:'Making and editing are two gears. A block is the gearbox jamming.',dek:'Creative work swings between a loose, dreamy generating mode and a sharp, logical editing one. Block is that switch getting stuck in one position.',read:'4 min read',aside:'Feeling “strangled” or feeling “all over the place” are two different jams, with two different fixes.',body:`<p>An old and durable model says making has two modes: a loose, associative, idea-generating one and a tight, critical, shaping one. Healthy work flips between them smoothly. Block is the flip getting stuck — either an over-strict inner editor strangling ideas before they form, or a flood of material with no editor to give it shape <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>Name which gear is stuck</h2>
<p>The value here is diagnostic. If a person feels choked — “I can’t get anything out” — the critical gear is jammed on, and the fix is to loosen it: play, freewriting, permission to be bad, a little self-kindness. If a person feels scattered — “I have everything and nothing” — the shaping gear is missing, and the fix is gentle structure and a dedicated editing phase.</p>
<blockquote>Modern research backs the skeleton: artists really do drop into the loose, dreamy mode more easily — better dream recall, more fantasy-proneness.</blockquote>
<p>You don’t need to lecture anyone on the theory. It just gives you language for what someone is feeling, and points the fix in the right direction.</p>
<div class="article-note">A model, not a mechanism proven in a lab. Useful for aiming an intervention, not for making claims about the brain.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Kris, E. (1952). <em>Psychoanalytic Explorations in Art</em> (the loosening/shaping modes); with support from Martindale (1999) on artists’ access to loose cognition.</p>
</div>`},

brainview:{k:'What’s happening',title:'The brain view of block — a useful guess, not a fact.',dek:'The neuroscience is honest enough to call itself a hypothesis. But it does tell you one important thing: when to stop coaching and refer.',read:'4 min read',aside:'If drive itself has collapsed, look at mood and health — not technique.',body:`<p>The best brain-level account we have is Alice Flaherty’s, and to her credit she flags it clearly as a hypothesis rather than settled fact <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. The sketch: block lives mostly in the front of the brain — planning and judging — plus the emotion centres. Idea-generation and idea-judging need to stay in balance; block is generation running too low, judgement running too harsh, or drive collapsing altogether.</p>
<h2>Why a guess still earns its place</h2>
<p>It doesn’t hand you a new tool — the fixes are the same as everywhere else. What it gives you is a boundary. If someone’s <em>drive</em> has genuinely gone — not “I’m avoiding it” but “there is no engine” — that can be depression, and depression is a health matter, not a coaching project <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>.</p>
<blockquote>The honest move, when the tank isn’t just low but the engine is gone, is to refer — not to design a cleverer exercise.</blockquote>
<p>We keep the brain view exactly where it belongs: background that keeps us humble, and a reminder of where our scope ends.</p>
<div class="article-note">Presented as a hypothesis on purpose. A real collapse of drive is a reason to involve qualified support.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Flaherty, A. W. (2005). Frontotemporal and dopaminergic control of idea generation and creative drive. <em>Journal of Comparative Neurology, 493</em>(1).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Flaherty, A. W. (2004). <em>The Midnight Disease</em>.</p>
</div>`},

scheduling:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'The most powerful unblocking tool is also the most boring.',dek:'Small, fixed, regular work sessions — done whether or not inspiration shows up — beat waiting for the muse by a factor most people don’t believe.',read:'6 min read',aside:'Scheduled writers didn’t just produce more. They had more creative ideas, not fewer.',body:`<p>If there’s a single strongest tool in the whole field, it’s this, and it’s deflating: schedule the work. Robert Boice’s studies pitted writers who worked in small, regular, fixed sessions against writers who waited for inspiration. The scheduled group produced roughly <strong>four times as much</strong> — and, crucially, generated <em>more</em> creative ideas, not fewer <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Inspiration, it turns out, mostly shows up once you’re already working.</p>
<h2>How to actually do it</h2>
<p>Keep the sessions tiny — 15 to 25 minutes — small enough that starting isn’t a negotiation. Put a reward after each one. Use a hard stop on the clock, even mid-flow, because stopping while it’s still good means tomorrow starts easy. A single daily one-line check-in keeps the thread alive between sessions.</p>
<blockquote>You are not forcing output. You are removing the daily decision about whether to start — which is where most of the energy leaks out.</blockquote>
<h2>Why it beats inspiration</h2>
<p>Scheduling works because it defeats the two biggest productivity killers at once: waiting to feel ready, and having no finish line in view. It also quietly retrains the avoidance loop — every completed small session is evidence that the desk is safe. This is the default engine for almost every Starting &amp; Doing block, and the base that the fancier tools sit on top of.</p>
<div class="article-note">Scheduling is for execution and avoidance, not for exhaustion. If someone is genuinely depleted, more sessions dig the hole deeper — recover first.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Boice, R. (1983, 1990) on scheduled vs. spontaneous writing and the appearance of creative ideas; see also <em>Professors as Writers</em>.</p>
</div>`},

thinking:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'Find the secret rule that’s freezing you, and break it.',dek:'A lot of blocks are one rigid, invisible rule treated as law. Name it, loosen it, and separate making from judging.',read:'5 min read',aside:'“The first line has to be perfect before I move on” is a rule, not a fact. It can be repealed.',body:`<p>Some blocks are cognitive: a flexible guideline has hardened into an unbreakable law, and you can’t proceed until you obey it. “The opening must be perfect first.” “Every chapter starts with action.” Mike Rose found these rigid rules jam people cold — and sometimes two rules contradict, so you freeze entirely <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>Catch the thought-errors</h2>
<p>The work is to surface the secret rule and consciously loosen it, while watching for the usual distortions riding alongside — catastrophising, over-generalising, predicting failure before you’ve started. And, always, separate drafting from editing so the two don’t compete.</p>
<blockquote>In the famous ceramics class, the group graded on quantity produced better pots than the group chasing one perfect piece. Volume teaches the hand faster than pressure does.</blockquote>
<h2>Quantity makes quality</h2>
<p>That’s the counter-intuitive engine of this fix: making a lot, badly, on purpose, produces better work than trying to make one flawless thing <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. It’s the direct antidote to the perfectionism and premature-editing that stall so many people. Loosen the rule, lower the stakes, and let volume do the teaching.</p>
<div class="article-note">Best for perfectionism, rigid-rule and premature-editing blocks. A rule that protects against a real risk deserves respect — loosen, don’t abolish blindly.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Rose, M. (1980). Rigid rules, inflexible plans, and the stifling of language. <em>CCC, 31</em>(4).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Bayles, D. &amp; Orland, T. (2011). <em>Art and Fear</em> (the quantity-vs-quality ceramics account).</p>
</div>`},

paradox:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'Tell a perfectionist to do it badly on purpose.',dek:'The reverse trick: when trying harder makes it worse, aim for the worst version you can manage. It’s a fix you literally cannot fail.',read:'4 min read',aside:'“Write the worst opening imaginable.” Either they can’t — or they’ve succeeded at being bad. Both mean they’re working.',body:`<p>For a certain kind of stuck person — the perfectionist frozen by an impossible standard — the ordinary advice makes it worse. So you flip it. Ask them to produce the <strong>worst</strong> version they possibly can <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>Why it can’t lose</h2>
<p>The instruction removes the very thing causing the freeze: the standard. Now there’s no way to fail. Either they genuinely try to be bad and succeed — in which case they were working — or they find they can’t help making something decent, in which case they’re working too. The “bad” draft is almost always far better than they feared, and now there’s something on the page to react to.</p>
<blockquote>You can’t be paralysed by the fear of not being good enough when the assignment is to be terrible.</blockquote>
<h2>When to reach for it</h2>
<p>Use it for perfectionism and starting blocks, especially the ones where effort and pressure only tighten the knot. It’s a small, almost playful intervention, but it breaks the specific trap — an unreachable standard — better than any amount of encouragement to “just relax and do your best.”</p>
<div class="article-note">A targeted tool for perfectionism-driven freezes, not a general method. If the block is depletion or a real skill gap, the paradox has nothing to work on.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Henning, L. H. (1981). Paradox as a treatment for writer’s block. <em>Personnel and Guidance Journal, 60</em>.</p>
</div>`},

emptychair:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'Give your inner critic a chair and let it talk.',dek:'When a loud internal voice keeps interrupting the work, one way through is to voice both sides of the fight out loud until something shifts.',read:'4 min read',aside:'Name the two parts — “the Critic” and “the one who wants to make” — and let them argue.',body:`<p>Plenty of blocks are an argument you’re having with yourself, just below hearing. One experiential fix borrowed from Gestalt work is to make the argument explicit: put the critic in a chair and let both sides speak out loud <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>How it runs</h2>
<p>You name the parts — say, the Critic and the part that wants to make something — and let them actually talk to each other. It sounds odd on paper; in practice, hearing the critic’s lines said aloud tends to drain their authority. Often the voice turns out to belong to someone specific — an old teacher, a parent, a review from years ago — and naming it is half the release.</p>
<blockquote>The goal isn’t to win the argument. It’s to learn to take your own side.</blockquote>
<h2>Who it suits</h2>
<p>It fits blocks driven by a loud inner critic, and people who respond to active, experiential work rather than quiet analysis. It matches one of the most consistent findings in the literature — that block is frequently an internalised voice interrupting you at the moment of creation <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="article-note">Best suited to Identity-flavoured blocks with a strong inner critic. Where that voice shades into something clinical, that’s a matter for qualified support.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Garcia, C., Baker, S. &amp; Demayo, R. (1999). Gestalt educational therapy for academic anxieties.</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Writer’s block revisited: a micro-phenomenological case study on an internalised voice (2017).</p>
</div>`},

imageryplay:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'When imagination goes flat, play beats effort.',dek:'For the empty, apathetic kind of block, guided imagery and childlike play do more than talking it through — and there’s data.',read:'5 min read',aside:'“Imagine you’re seven with a free afternoon and no one watching.” It measurably raises originality.',body:`<p>Not every block is anxious. Some are flat — the imagination has gone quiet and nothing feels interesting. For that apathetic, empty kind, the counter-intuitive finding is that <strong>imagery and play</strong> outperform talk <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>What it looks like</h2>
<p>Guided visualising of the stalled project; imagination games; the deliberately childlike prompt — “imagine you’re seven, with a free day and permission to make a mess.” In Barrios &amp; Singer’s comparison, waking imagery beat rational discussion at unblocking people, and worked best for exactly the apathetic type who’d gone flat <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Later work showed the seven-year-old prompt reliably raises the originality of what people produce <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>.</p>
<blockquote>Do what you want, not what you should. For a flat imagination, that instruction is medicine, not indulgence.</blockquote>
<h2>Why it works</h2>
<p>Play lowers the stakes and reopens the loose, generative gear that pressure had shut down. You’re not trying to produce anything good yet — you’re reminding the system that making can feel free. Once the spark is back, structure can follow.</p>
<div class="article-note">Aimed at the empty/apathetic block. If the flatness is persistent and total, screen gently for depletion or low mood before prescribing more play.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Barrios, M. V. &amp; Singer, J. L. (1981). The treatment of creative blocks. <em>Imagination, Cognition and Personality, 1</em>(1).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Zabelina, D. L. &amp; Robinson, M. D. (2010). Child’s play: facilitating originality via a priming manipulation. <em>Psychology of Aesthetics, Creativity, and the Arts, 4</em>(1).</p>
</div>`},

selfcompassion:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'Being kinder to yourself makes you more original — and it’s measured.',dek:'Self-compassion isn’t a soft add-on. In the people who need it most, a fifteen-minute exercise raised creative originality.',read:'5 min read',aside:'It helped the highly self-critical — which is precisely who’s stuck.',body:`<p>Self-criticism kills originality: it eats the workspace, and the fear of being bad crowds out the ideas. The evidence-backed counter is self-compassion — and it’s not vague niceness, it’s a specific, short practice with data behind it <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>The exercise</h2>
<p>Roughly fifteen minutes, three moves: notice that others feel this same failure (you’re not uniquely broken); write to yourself the way you’d write to a friend in the same spot; then view the situation from a calm distance <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. That’s it.</p>
<blockquote>In the study, it raised creative originality — but only in the highly self-critical people. Which is exactly the group that gets blocked.</blockquote>
<h2>Turning the critic down, not off</h2>
<p>The point isn’t to silence the editor — you need it later. It’s to lower its volume enough that ideas can form in the first place. For perfectionists and Identity blocks, where worth has fused with the work, this is one of the few interventions that targets the actual mechanism instead of nagging at the symptom.</p>
<div class="article-note">Strong for self-critical clients and perfectionists. It complements the editor; it doesn’t replace the judgement the work eventually needs.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Neff, K. D. (2003). Self-compassion: an alternative conceptualization of a healthy attitude toward oneself. <em>Self and Identity, 2</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Zabelina, D. L. &amp; Robinson, M. D. (2010). Don’t be so hard on yourself: self-compassion facilitates creative originality among self-judgmental individuals. <em>Creativity Research Journal, 22</em>(3).</p>
</div>`},

mindfulness:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'You don’t have to obey every thought you have.',dek:'Mindfulness won’t make you a genius. But it trains the one skill a blocked person needs: noticing the critical voice without freezing at it.',read:'4 min read',aside:'The point isn’t emptying your mind. It’s watching a thought pass instead of doing what it says.',body:`<p>Mindfulness gets oversold, so let’s be modest about it. What it actually trains is the ability to watch your own thoughts without automatically obeying them — and for a blocked person, that’s the whole game <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>What it does for a block</h2>
<p>When the critical voice starts up mid-work, the untrained response is to freeze or flee. The trained response is to notice it — “there’s the thought that this is rubbish” — and let it pass without downing tools. Reviews of the research are mixed but generally positive, with no harm found; regular practice tends to loosen habitual thinking and nudge up openness to new ideas <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<blockquote>Even beginners get some benefit. You’re not chasing enlightenment — you’re building a small gap between a thought and your reaction to it.</blockquote>
<h2>Where it fits</h2>
<p>It’s most useful for the self-judging loop and for flooded, anxious people who need to down-shift before they can work. Treat it as a supporting habit, not a headline cure — it makes the other tools land better.</p>
<div class="article-note">Modest, low-risk, supportive. Not a treatment for clinical anxiety or depression on its own.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Horan, R. (2009). The neuropsychological connection between creativity and meditation (review); consistent with broader mindfulness reviews.</p>
</div>`},

breaks:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'Walk, nap, sleep on it: the science of the productive break.',dek:'Stepping away isn’t slacking — done right, it’s one of the best-evidenced ways to break a stuck problem loose.',read:'5 min read',aside:'A walk boosts idea output by roughly 60%. But only after you’ve genuinely hit the wall.',body:`<p>When you’re fixated on a specific problem in the work, the fix isn’t more staring — it’s a deliberate, well-timed break. The catch is that breaks only work under conditions, which is why “just take a walk” sometimes helps and sometimes doesn’t <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>How to break properly</h2>
<p>First, do real work and genuinely hit the impasse — incubation has nothing to incubate otherwise. Then step away into something low-effort: a walk is best, boosting idea output by around 60% <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. Sleep on genuinely hard problems — a night’s sleep roughly doubles the odds of insight <sup><a href="#ref-c">(c)</a></sup>. And keep several pieces going so you can rotate when one jams.</p>
<blockquote>A useful pause has a relationship to the work before and after it. Effort, then ease, then return.</blockquote>
<h2>Why it beats grinding</h2>
<p>The break lets the wrong, fixated approach fade so a fresher one can surface — the shower-thought effect, formalised. It’s the specific antidote to a Process block, where the problem is in the work itself and pushing only deepens the rut.</p>
<div class="article-note">For fixation and craft problems. If someone can’t ever return from the break, that’s avoidance wearing incubation’s clothes — a different issue.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Sio, U. N. &amp; Ormerod, T. C. (2009). Does incubation enhance problem solving? <em>Psychological Bulletin, 135</em>(1).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Oppezzo, M. &amp; Schwartz, D. L. (2014). Give your ideas some legs: walking and creative thinking. <em>JEP: LMC, 40</em>(4).</p>
<p id="ref-c"><strong>(c)</strong> Wagner, U. et al. (2004). Sleep inspires insight. <em>Nature, 427</em>.</p>
</div>`},

people:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'Needing other people isn’t weakness. It’s load-bearing.',dek:'The loudest theme in the whole literature is the quietest to admit: creative work runs on relationships, and isolation blocks it.',read:'5 min read',aside:'Find three: a Mirror, a Hero, a Twin. Start with the Twin — they’re the easiest to find.',body:`<p>Across the entire field, the most consistent theme is also the least fashionable: recovery from block needs other people <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Isolation is one of the biggest documented drags on an artist’s best work — and “needing support” is a structural fact of creative work, not a character flaw.</p>
<h2>Three kinds of person</h2>
<p>Anne Paris names three roles worth having <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. A <strong>Mirror</strong> reflects your strengths back when you can’t see them yourself. A <strong>Hero</strong> is someone a little further ahead whose example lends you courage. A <strong>Twin</strong> is a peer in the same fight — the easiest to find, so start there. When you ask for support, ask precisely: “tell me what’s working here,” not “what do you think?”</p>
<blockquote>When you genuinely can’t see your own qualities, the fix often isn’t internal at all. It’s a mirror.</blockquote>
<h2>Why it’s a real intervention</h2>
<p>Gardner found recovery needed both comfort <em>and</em> someone who truly understood the work <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. That’s why this is a Context &amp; Support fix, not a nice-to-have — and why it’s the natural strength of coaching. Some blocks lift the moment the right person is in the room.</p>
<div class="article-note">For isolation and Context &amp; Support blocks, and low self-efficacy that no amount of solo effort shifts.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Gardner, H. (1993). <em>Creating Minds</em>; Dewey, Steinberg &amp; Coulson (1998) on isolation.</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Paris, A. (2008). <em>Standing at Water’s Edge</em> (mirrors, heroes, twins).</p>
</div>`},

goodsessions:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'What good help for a block actually looks like.',dek:'Diagnose before you dig. Support before you challenge. Treat the cause, not the symptom — and never open with the list of what’s wrong.',read:'5 min read',aside:'The single best question: “If I fixed one thing tonight and you’d work tomorrow, what is it?”',body:`<p>The specific tools only work inside the right frame. Drawn from the clinicians who’ve spent careers on this, the shape of good help is fairly consistent — and mostly the opposite of “here’s your problem, now fix it” <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>The shape of it</h2>
<p>Diagnose first — never assume the block is what it looks like. Build support before digging into anything painful. Treat causes, not just symptoms. Reflect small wins back, and don’t open a session with the list of everything wrong. Protect the work itself — no premature reviews. Sanction real rest when that’s what’s needed. And refer out the moment it’s clinical.</p>
<blockquote>People name their real block without realising it. Ask what single thing, fixed overnight, would let them work tomorrow — then ask what makes that hard.</blockquote>
<h2>Why the frame matters</h2>
<p>A blocked person is usually already their own harshest critic; leading with the problem list just confirms the story that’s keeping them stuck. Starting from strengths and a real diagnosis is what makes the tools land at all. This is less a technique than the posture everything else sits inside.</p>
<div class="article-note">This is method, not a cure-all. The referral line is part of it: some things belong with a qualified clinician, and saying so is good practice.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Synthesised from Gallay (2013) and her field consultants (Wolson, Bews, Paris, Maisel); Kantor (1995); Lowman (1993).</p>
</div>`},

dangermoments:{k:'Fixes with evidence',title:'Blocks strike at predictable moments. Guard them.',dek:'Finishing, starting, right after a success, right after a big goal, in a dry spell — these are the ambush points. You can build defences in advance.',read:'5 min read',aside:'Keep new work private for a while before showing it. The exposed moment is when confidence is thinnest.',body:`<p>Blocks aren’t random. They cluster at a handful of predictable danger points, which means you can prevent a lot of them by building defences into the routine rather than reacting once you’re already stuck <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>The ambush points</h2>
<p><strong>Finishing:</strong> keep new work private for an insulating period before you show it — the moment of exposure is when a tepid reaction does the most damage. <strong>Starting:</strong> lower the stakes so beginning isn’t a verdict. <strong>After a success:</strong> re-enter with small, low-stakes work instead of trying to top yourself. <strong>After a big goal is reached:</strong> line up the next one <em>before</em> you finish, so you don’t fall into the “now what?” void. <strong>In a drought:</strong> rotate projects and play with technique rather than forcing the dry piece.</p>
<blockquote>Prevention is cheaper than rescue. Most recurring blocks are a predictable moment that nobody planned for.</blockquote>
<h2>Build it into the routine</h2>
<p>None of these are heroic. They’re small habits placed at the exact points where blocks tend to form — an insulating period, a low-stakes re-entry, a next goal queued up. Put them in ahead of time and the same block stops recurring.</p>
<div class="article-note">Preventive, not a treatment for an active severe block. Pair with the diagnosis when a block is already in full swing.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Bayles, D. &amp; Orland, T. (2011). <em>Art and Fear</em> (the insulating period and the fear list).</p>
</div>`},

howcommon:{k:'Foundations',title:'Almost everyone gets blocked. Almost no one has studied it.',dek:'The numbers are striking and the research is nearly empty. And it turns out block isn’t an artist thing at all — it’s an ill-defined-problem thing.',read:'6 min read',aside:'78% of professionally active artists reported it. The field could find two empirical studies on it.',body:`<p>Two facts sit uncomfortably next to each other. In Crosson’s survey of 271 professionally active artists and writers, <strong>78% reported experiencing creative block</strong> <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. And when Gallay searched the databases for empirical research focused on block in professional artists, she found only <strong>two</strong> studies — both decades old <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. Something this common is almost entirely unstudied.</p>
<h2>The darkest statistic</h2>
<p>Bayles &amp; Orland point at the bleakest number of all: the great majority of people who start an art education or career quit entirely — which you can read as <em>permanent block</em>, the most severe outcome on the spectrum <sup><a href="#ref-c">(c)</a></sup>. The block that ends a practice rarely announces itself; it just quietly wins.</p>
<h2>Why so neglected?</h2>
<p>Block is a genuinely hard research target — intrapsychic, interpersonal, situational and historical factors braided differently in every person, with no agreed measuring tools and messy, mixed samples. Add the stereotype that artists are mysterious rather than studyable, and the science stalled while a hundred self-help books rushed the gap <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>Why there’s no “doctor’s block”</h2>
<p>Here’s the part that widens it out. We never hear about accountant’s block or doctor’s block, because those jobs are <strong>well-defined problems with known procedures</strong> — you know what to do, you just do it <sup><a href="#ref-d">(d)</a></sup>. Block lives in <em>ill-defined</em> problems, where you have to find and define the problem before you can solve it.</p>
<blockquote>Career block, founder block, someone stuck reinventing their own direction — that’s creative block wearing a different outfit. Same mechanism: skill, desire, stuckness and suffering, all at once.</blockquote>
<p>Which is the whole reason we don’t treat this as an art-school problem. Anyone building something into existence, in that open and undefined space, can get blocked there.</p>
<div class="article-note">The 78% describes one sample of artists and writers, not every profession. We use the figure carefully and say where it doesn’t generalise.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Crosson, C. W. (1982). Creative block: A brief inquiry. <em>The Arts in Psychotherapy, 9</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Gallay, L. H. (2013). <em>Understanding and Treating Creative Block in Professional Artists</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-c"><strong>(c)</strong> Bayles, D. &amp; Orland, T. (2011). <em>Art and Fear</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-d"><strong>(d)</strong> Flaherty, A. W. (2004). <em>The Midnight Disease</em> (block as a disorder of ill-defined problems).</p>
</div>`},

found7plus1:{k:'Our framework',title:'How we turned a messy pile of research into seven doors and one trapdoor.',dek:'There’s no official taxonomy of creative block. So we built a working one — seven practical domains plus a look-alike — judged by whether it helps, not whether it matches a rulebook that doesn’t exist.',read:'7 min read',aside:'Seven true domains, plus a Skill Gap that only looks like a block.',body:`<p>Start with an admission: there is no agreed scientific taxonomy of block types. As Gallay puts it, there are as many classifications as there have been explanations of the cause <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. That could be paralysing. We took it as permission to build a map judged by one test — does it help a real person get unstuck?</p>
<h2>Collapsing the pile</h2>
<p>We lined up the serious typologies — Crosson’s six cause-categories, Barrios &amp; Singer’s four personality profiles, the five cause-families, the situational blocks — and looked for the smallest set of <em>practical</em> domains they kept pointing at <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Seven kept surviving the merge:</p>
<p><strong>Starting &amp; Doing</strong> (execution), <strong>Confidence &amp; Exposure</strong> (identity), <strong>Direction &amp; Meaning</strong> (direction), <strong>Energy &amp; Recovery</strong> (depletion), <strong>The Work Itself</strong> (process), <strong>Structure &amp; Workflow</strong> (systems), and <strong>Context &amp; Support</strong> (the world around the work).</p>
<h2>The trapdoor: +1</h2>
<p>Then there’s the one that isn’t really a block at all. By the strict definition, being stuck for lack of skill is a <em>skill gap</em>, not a block <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. But it disguises itself as one beautifully — so we keep it on the map as a “+1” look-alike, precisely so we don’t spend months psychologising a problem that a course would fix.</p>
<blockquote>Most people are a mix, with one domain pulling hardest right now. Naming that one honestly — not conveniently — is most of the work.</blockquote>
<h2>Why domains, not types</h2>
<p>They’re deliberately not personality types or diagnoses. They’re working hypotheses that sit on top of your <em>terrain</em> — the stable and changeable factors underneath — and they’re allowed to overlap and to be wrong. The moment better evidence arrives, the map is supposed to change. That’s the difference between a framework and a horoscope.</p>
<div class="article-note">A practitioner map, not a validated diagnostic system. The domains earn their place by being useful, and lose it when they aren’t.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Gallay, L. H. (2013); Crosson (1982); Barrios &amp; Singer (1981) — the source typologies synthesised.</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Rose, M. (1984). <em>Writer’s Block: The Cognitive Dimension</em> (block vs. skill deficit).</p>
</div>`},

states:{k:'The state dial',title:'Same block, opposite fix: are you flooded, empty, or regulated?',dek:'Two people with the identical block can need opposite first moves. Read the state before you push for output — get it backwards and you deepen the block.',read:'6 min read',aside:'Flooded needs less force. Empty needs more than “rest”. Regulated is ready for the real work.',body:`<p>Before the block type even matters, there’s a prior question: what state is the person in? Performance follows an upside-down U — too little activation and there’s no drive or focus; too much and the thinking brain goes offline and you flee the desk <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Creative work has a low ceiling on that curve, which is why it’s so easy to get knocked out of it.</p>
<h2>Flooded needs less force</h2>
<p>High activation feels like panic, urgency, a need to escape. Push harder here and you just increase the rigidity. The move is to <em>regulate</em>: shorter sessions, smaller stakes, a walk, orienting to the room, making the task smaller until it’s approachable again.</p>
<h2>Empty needs more than a slogan</h2>
<p>Low activation feels flat, heavy, fuel-less. But “just rest” is lazy advice — empty can mean genuine depletion, lost interest, low confidence, or too much load, and each wants a different response: real recovery, gentle activation, meaningful contact, or an honest look at whether the project still matters <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>.</p>
<blockquote>Cool the flooded; activate the empty. Give the wrong medicine — pressure to someone empty, or more push to someone flooded — and the block gets worse.</blockquote>
<h2>Not an eighth block</h2>
<p>State is a dial, not another category. It doesn’t tell us <em>what</em> the block is; it tells us <em>how</em> to begin. Regulated doesn’t mean perfectly calm, either — just available enough to engage. Read the dial first, and the same tool suddenly points the right way.</p>
<div class="article-note">Persistent emptiness or flooding that won’t shift can be depression or an anxiety condition — a matter for qualified support, not a harder push.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> The Yerkes–Dodson arousal curve, applied to creativity in Flaherty, A. W. (2004). <em>The Midnight Disease</em>.</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Baas, De Dreu &amp; Nijstad (2008), on activation and mood; Flaherty (2004) on high- vs low-energy block.</p>
</div>`},

craftvsgrow:{k:'Our method',title:'Why we don’t use GROW — and built CRAFT instead.',dek:'The classic coaching model assumes you already know your goal and just need the will. A blocked person often has neither. So we start somewhere else.',read:'6 min read',aside:'GROW mobilises a willing person toward a known goal. Block breaks both of those assumptions.',body:`<p>The default coaching model, GROW — Goal, Reality, Options, Will — is genuinely good at what it’s for: helping a motivated person move toward a goal they can already name. It assumes two things are in place: a clear goal, and the will to pursue it. A blocked person frequently has <strong>neither</strong> — which is exactly why GROW so often spins its wheels on them.</p>
<h2>Where GROW runs aground</h2>
<p>Ask a blocked person for their goal and you may hit a Direction block — there is no clear goal yet, and “set a SMART one” misses the point. Ask them to summon the will and you ignore that they might be flooded, empty, or fused with a fear they can’t out-will. GROW has no diagnostic step and no place to regulate a nervous system. It was built for performance, not for stuckness.</p>
<h2>What CRAFT adds</h2>
<p>So our method starts earlier and stays honest about state. <strong>Clarify</strong> — what’s actually stuck, and is this even a block? <strong>Regulate</strong> — make the person workable before demanding output. <strong>Address</strong> — choose a response that fits the likely mechanism. <strong>Form</strong> — turn insight into a concrete experiment or change. <strong>Test</strong> — try it in real work and adjust.</p>
<blockquote>GROW asks “what do you want and will you do it?” CRAFT asks “what’s really going on, and are you in any state to move?” — first.</blockquote>
<h2>One more thing</h2>
<p>CRAFT is a flexible loop, not a staircase — you form a hypothesis, test it, and revise when the evidence changes. And a note on the name: CRAFT the method is not the same as “craft” the block (a problem in the work itself). Same word, different jobs.</p>
<div class="article-note">This isn’t a knock on GROW — it’s about fit. For a clear-goal, willing client, GROW is fine. For a block, the diagnosis and the state come first.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> GROW model, Whitmore, J., <em>Coaching for Performance</em>. CRAFT is A Creative Block’s own session method, synthesised from the diagnostic and treatment literature in Gallay (2013).</p>
</div>`},

adhd:{k:'The terrain',title:'ADHD and creative block: when the problem is the engine, not the will.',dek:'For an ADHD brain, “just start” is the exact instruction that doesn’t compute. Understanding the wiring changes the whole approach — and there’s a method built for it.',read:'6 min read',aside:'ADHD isn’t a block. It’s terrain a block can grow on — and it’s terrain you design around, not against.',body:`<p>On our map, ADHD isn’t a block — it’s <strong>terrain</strong>. It sits in the stable, wired-in factors underneath, and it strongly shapes which blocks form and how you should respond to them <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Treating an ADHD execution problem as a motivation failure is one of the most common and most damaging mistakes in this whole area.</p>
<h2>Why “just start” doesn’t compute</h2>
<p>ADHD is, at heart, a difference in executive function — task initiation, working memory, and the felt sense of time <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Motivation tends to be interest- and urgency-driven rather than importance-driven, which is why a boring-but-important task can be genuinely, physically hard to begin, while a fascinating one triggers hyperfocus for six hours. From outside it looks like inconsistency or avoidance. Inside, it’s an engine that idles and surges on its own logic.</p>
<blockquote>The block here is usually Starting &amp; Doing — but its root is wiring, not willpower. Shame-based advice doesn’t just miss; it actively makes it worse.</blockquote>
<h2>Design around the wiring</h2>
<p>The response is to build scaffolding the brain will actually use: tiny, externally-timed sessions; a single visible finish line; immediate rewards; body-doubling and accountability; systems with almost no moving parts, because elaborate ones collapse within days. Notably, this is also the terrain where the scheduling and avoidance research pays off most — short, rewarded, timed reps are close to purpose-built for an ADHD brain.</p>
<h2>A method built for it</h2>
<p>It’s no accident that David Cain designed his Block Method after his own ADHD diagnosis, explicitly for people who’ve failed at the complex systems in the famous productivity books <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. Minimal parts, 25-minute blocks, a visible tally — it’s a good example of designing <em>with</em> the wiring instead of against it.</p>
<div class="article-note">ADHD is a clinical condition; this is not diagnosis or treatment. Where it’s suspected or unmanaged, the right first step is a qualified professional — coaching designs around it, it doesn’t replace care.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Executive-function and time-perception models of ADHD (e.g. Barkley), on initiation, working memory and delay.</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Cain, D. (2021). <em>How to Do Things: Productivity for the Productivity-Challenged</em>.</p>
</div>`},

cain:{k:'Tools that work',title:'David Cain’s Block Method: productivity for people who hate productivity systems.',dek:'Most productivity books are written by high achievers for high achievers. This one was built by someone who kept failing at them — and it’s beautifully simple.',read:'6 min read',aside:'Think of each 25-minute work session as a small, solid wooden box. Make boxes. Stack them.',body:`<p>Most productivity systems assume the hard part is organising your tasks, and that once they’re slotted neatly into place you’ll simply do them. For a lot of people — anyone who’s bounced off <em>Getting Things Done</em> or <em>The Pomodoro Technique</em> — that assumption is exactly backwards. The doing <em>is</em> the hard part. David Cain, who built his method after an ADHD diagnosis at 40, designed for precisely that gap <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>The method, in one breath</h2>
<p>You need three things: a timer, a rough list of tasks, and somewhere to mark what you finish. Then, for each <strong>Block</strong>: pick a task, briefly picture its finish line, work toward it with unwavering intention for 25 minutes, and mark one completed Block on your scoresheet — with a little care and gratitude <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. That’s the whole thing.</p>
<blockquote>Picture each Block as a small, well-made wooden box — sanded, solid, the same size every time. You’re not surviving the day. You’re making boxes and stacking them into a life.</blockquote>
<h2>Why it works so well</h2>
<p>The metaphor isn’t decoration — it makes an abstract chunk of effort feel like a real, valuable object, which is oddly motivating. Working in Blocks also kills the two big leaks instantly: no finish line, and “quick” email checks that shatter focus. Then you improve on three axes — <strong>quantity</strong> (more Blocks), <strong>quality</strong> (denser ones), and <strong>consistency</strong> (routines you can sustain).</p>
<h2>Why it beats the big systems</h2>
<p>Its whole advantage is having almost no moving parts. Elaborate systems of interlocking lists and folders fall apart within 48 hours for exactly the people who need help most; a method with one timer and a sticky note survives. As Cain puts it, the power of a good idea is in the <em>implementation</em>, not the information — and this is about the most implementable version there is.</p>
<div class="article-note">It won’t cure the thing underneath — it won’t fix ADHD or make you a natural go-getter. It helps you be as productive as you can be in spite of it, which is usually a lot more than before.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Cain, D. (2021). <em>How to Do Things: Productivity for the Productivity-Challenged</em>, v1.0.</p>
</div>`},

finish:{k:'What’s happening',title:'Why finishing can feel more dangerous than starting.',dek:'Exposure, judgement and self-worth get loudest exactly when the work is nearly ready. The last ten percent becomes the scariest part.',read:'6 min read',aside:'The pattern: work reaches 80–95%, then the revising, restarting, or quiet disappearing begins.',body:`<p>An unfinished project is still full of possibility. A finished one can be judged. That single shift is why, for a lot of people, the final stretch is the most threatening part of the whole process — and why so much work stalls right before it would be seen <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>When the artefact becomes the self</h2>
<p>Bayles &amp; Orland put the mechanism in one uncomfortable sentence: an artist’s sense of self fuses with the work, so “fears of not making good enough art turn into fears of not being good enough” as a person <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. Once that’s true, polishing isn’t only about quality — it’s protection from a verdict. Finishing means finding out, and not-finishing keeps the answer safely unknown.</p>
<blockquote>The goal is not fearless exposure. It’s tolerable exposure — enough safety to let the thing be seen.</blockquote>
<h2>The anxious perfectionist</h2>
<p>This is the territory of Barrios &amp; Singer’s anxious self-critic — paralysed by their own standards, unable to call anything done <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. And it has a predictable danger point: the moment of showing new work, when a tepid reaction lands hardest. Hirst’s artists described exactly this — a paralysing inability to make the final decisions, right at the end <sup><a href="#ref-c">(c)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>Make exposure survivable</h2>
<p>The response isn’t a pep talk about confidence. It’s practical: define what “done enough” means <em>before</em> the final stretch, so the finish line isn’t a moving target. Ask for specific feedback, not a global verdict. Share in graduated steps rather than one big reveal. And keep the person larger than the artefact — the work is a thing you made, not a referendum on your worth.</p>
<div class="article-note">This is the Confidence &amp; Exposure (Identity) block. Where the fear is severe or tied to something clinical, that’s a matter for qualified support, not just a smaller reveal.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Bayles, D. &amp; Orland, T. (2011). <em>Art and Fear</em> (the fear list and the insulating period).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Barrios, M. V. &amp; Singer, J. L. (1981). The treatment of creative blocks. <em>Imagination, Cognition and Personality, 1</em>(1).</p>
<p id="ref-c"><strong>(c)</strong> Hirst, B. (1992). How artists overcome creative blocks (post-completion blocks; decision paralysis).</p>
</div>`},

toobig:{k:'What’s happening',title:'When the project is too big, motivation isn’t the first problem.',dek:'Scope, sequencing and decision load can quietly eat the energy you needed to start. It feels personal from the inside — but it’s a structure problem.',read:'5 min read',aside:'“Work on the project” is not a next action. It’s a request to rebuild the whole map first.',body:`<p>A project with no visible boundaries forces your brain to rebuild the entire map every time you approach it — where things stand, what depends on what, what to do first. Half your available energy is gone before any making begins. From the inside this reads as a motivation failure. It’s usually a systems failure <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<h2>The hidden tax of scope</h2>
<p>Swollen scope, unclear sequencing, decisions that keep reopening, an unreliable workflow — each one adds friction that has nothing to do with how much you care. You can be fully motivated and still blocked, simply because the container the work has to live in keeps generating the same drag <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>.</p>
<blockquote>A good system doesn’t control creativity. It protects your attention for the parts that actually need creative judgement.</blockquote>
<h2>Turn a project into a sequence</h2>
<p>The fix is to make the thing legible. Define the outcome. Reduce the <em>active</em> scope to what’s in front of you. Expose the dependencies so they stop ambushing you. Separate decisions from actions — deciding and doing are different jobs and shouldn’t compete <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>. And give every work session a default entry point, so starting is never a fresh negotiation.</p>
<div class="article-note">This is the Structure &amp; Workflow (Systems) block. It shouldn’t be psychologised — but identity, avoidance or context can still affect whether a better system actually gets used.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Boice, R. (1985). Cognitive components of blocking, including planning problems. <em>Written Communication, 2</em>(1).</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Rose, M. (1980). Rigid rules, inflexible plans, and the stifling of language. <em>CCC, 31</em>(4).</p>
</div>`},

feedback:{k:'What’s happening',title:'Bad feedback blocks good work. Better feedback is designed.',dek:'Vague judgement, being watched, and too many decision-makers all shrink exploration. Feedback isn’t a neutral event — it’s part of the creative environment.',read:'6 min read',aside:'“I don’t like it” hands the maker a social threat and no usable information.',body:`<p>Creativity is situated — it happens inside conditions, not in a vacuum. And one of the most powerful conditions is how the work gets evaluated. Expected evaluation, surveillance and competition reliably narrow the search for original ideas <sup><a href="#ref-a">(a)</a></sup>. So feedback isn’t a neutral checkpoint; it’s an ingredient that can enable a project or quietly block it.</p>
<h2>Why vague judgement does damage</h2>
<p>“I don’t like it” gives you a social threat and nothing to act on. Repeated exposure to that kind of global evaluation doesn’t just sting in the moment — it can install an internal critic that keeps talking long after the meeting ends. Add the art world’s no-win double-bind messages — “be original, but make it sell” — and people end up self-critical without realising they’re reacting to the room, not the work <sup><a href="#ref-b">(b)</a></sup>.</p>
<blockquote>Specific critique improves the work. Global judgement just makes the person defend themselves.</blockquote>
<h2>Give feedback one job</h2>
<p>Designed feedback is clearer, not softer. Name the stage, the decision being made, and the criteria that matter <em>now</em>. Separate generative feedback (help me make it better) from approval (is this allowed to ship). Reduce the number of voices with real decision power. And build teams that have room for unfinished thinking plus clarity about who actually decides <sup><a href="#ref-c">(c)</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="article-note">This is the Context &amp; Support block — often one of the fastest to shift, because environment is so changeable. We try not to ask individuals to endlessly adapt to a system that keeps producing the problem.</div>
<div class="article-refs"><h3>References:</h3>
<p id="ref-a"><strong>(a)</strong> Amabile, T. M. et al. (1994); Hennessey, B. A. &amp; Amabile, T. M. (2010), on expected evaluation and creativity.</p>
<p id="ref-b"><strong>(b)</strong> Kantor, M. (1995); Plotnik, A. (2012), on double-bind messages.</p>
<p id="ref-c"><strong>(c)</strong> Csikszentmihályi, M. (1997); Dewey, Steinberg &amp; Coulson (1998), on the field, evaluation climate and conditions for best work.</p>
</div>`}

};
const key=document.body.dataset.article,a=ARTICLES[key]||ARTICLES.sixfactors;document.title=a.title+' — A Creative Block';document.querySelector('main[data-article]').innerHTML=`<style>.article-copy strong{font-weight:900;text-transform:uppercase}.article-copy .article-refs strong{font-weight:700;text-transform:none;color:#FF7F50}.article-copy .article-refs{padding-top:1.8em}.article-copy .article-refs h3{margin-bottom:.7em}.article-copy .article-refs p{font-size:11px;margin-top:0}.article-copy sup a,.article-copy sup{color:#FF7F50}.article-copy .article-note{background:#FF7F50}</style><section class="article-head"><div class="wrap"><div class="article-kicker">${a.k}</div><div><h1>${a.title}</h1><p class="article-dek">${a.dek}</p><p class="article-meta">A Creative Block · ${a.read}</p></div></div></section><section class="article-body"><div class="wrap article-layout"><aside class="article-aside">${a.aside}<p style="margin-top:24px"><a href="blog.html">← All articles</a></p></aside><article class="article-copy">${a.body}</article></div></section>`;
